Caused by k87 (0978563) and spotted by the agent that caused it.

t/type_model.t line 75:

  for my $leak (qw( croak blessed )) {
    my @found = grep { $_->can($leak) } @classes;
    is_deeply \@found, [], "no generated class answers ->$leak";
  }

The comment above it says: "Drop the namespace::clean line from
API::Docker::Role::Type and this fails 201 times (karr k82)." That is still
true of the `croak` half. It is no longer true of the `blessed` half: k87
removed the blessed import from Role::Type, so that name cannot leak from
there whatever the namespace::clean line does. The assertion still passes,
and it now proves nothing.

This is the repo's own rule 7 -- a test that cannot fail when the logic
changes is wrong -- landing on a test written to enforce rule 7.

The cheap fix is to drop `blessed` from the list and say why. The better one
is to stop blacklisting names at all and assert the positive: a generated
class's method surface is its registry attributes plus the documented handful
from the role, and nothing else. That version cannot go vacuous, and it would
have caught both croak and blessed without anyone naming them -- but check
what it costs on 201 classes before committing to it.

Whichever is chosen, mutation-check it: remove the namespace::clean line
again and confirm the test fails.

Done in 17b5365, and the agent took the harder option: the two-name blacklist is gone, replaced by a positive roster. It measured first -- across all 201 classes the non-attribute symbols are the same 33 names with no per-class variation -- so the roster is one literal list grouped by origin, asserted in BOTH directions, so it fails on a name that appears and on one that disappears. Cost is under a second. For the DSL package it used origin instead of a name list (B::svref_2object -> STASH -> NAME), because there the invariant really is 'nothing foreign lives here', which survives a rename. Three mutation probes, run in a throwaway git archive tree so k80's dirty working copy could not leak in. The third is the one that justifies the whole rewrite: an import placed AFTER the namespace::clean line, the line left in place -- the old blacklist passed, the roster names the leak. It caught something nobody had thought of.
